A||N||10||Art Doyle||Preserving Fieros (wear avoidance tips) ?||||5||
Z||000000||Art Doyle||11-21-2001||11:11 PM||||I'm new to 1988 Fieros, but have already developed a deep respect for their ability to suck money from one's wallet. Accordingly, I'd like to accumulate some tips from many of you old pros as to how total life cycle costs can be kept to a minimum through proper maintenance.<P>For instance, how could one pack the front wheel bearing assembly with new grease, how does one best prevent the balljoints from wearing out?<P>I am looking for "gotchas" which many of you now know by heart - but perhaps don't realize that the rest of us are still facing those same "dues".<P>Ideally, this thread will develop into a repository for your wisdom and experience. A means of sharing your hard won "lessons" with the rest of us. The objective of this exercise is simple - to keep the Fiero running well into the twenty first century.||||reg||5
Z||000001||avengador1||11-22-2001||10:44 AM||||Lock it up in an enviromentally controlled room, up on blocks and never use it. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Just kidding. Drive the car in good weather only, use high quality car wax, keep up with routine maintenance, replace worn parts, use seat covers,don't park close to SUV's, keep car garaged, use common sense, etc. Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierobruiser||11-22-2001||11:09 AM||||Know what you mean. My wife alternately calls my 88 the Succubus, the Black Hole, and the 'Fiero that ate the roof'. I bought it used of course with 90,000. Before marriage and children I owned new  a 1965 Sunbeam Tiger and a 1970 240Z. Since marriage it has been station wagons that I almost always followed the drill of buying a used car with about 80K and running it until it started to need things and then dumping it and getting another 80k cheapy. Maybe I just never kept a car long enough to know exactly how needy a hi-miles car can be but I'm learning with my daily driver Fiero. Eventually it'll be almost all new again.<P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||11-22-2001||05:11 PM||||If driving the car daily. It would probably be a good idea to get it rust undercoated. Fieros rust out just as easily as any car, they jsut don't show it tell its starts to get really bad!||||reg||4
Z||000004||Mickey_Moose||11-22-2001||06:27 PM||||well one thing I can tell you about the front wheel bearings, is that you can't really repack them with grease...you can get at the inner most one by removing the unit from the car and popping off the dust cover, but there is no real way to get at the other bearing ;(<P><BR>one other thing to note here, is that they are a discontinued item, no one seems to be making them anymore - yes I have tried Autozone.com to those that are going to suggest it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Tim<P>||||reg||9
Z||000005||deceler8||11-22-2001||08:36 PM||||The front bearings repack just like any car...you need the seals for the front because you'll destroy the old ones removing them to get at the inner bearings. Just pop out the seals with a screwdriver or seal puller and the bearings come right out.<P>It's the rears that are permamently sealed assemblies. If someone knows how to regrease those, I'd like to know.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/deceler8_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||11-22-2001||08:53 PM||||If I'm not mistaken, the 88 bearings are a sealed cartridge, correct? I wasn't aware of ANY way to repack them  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>As for BJs, avoid rough roads, and hitting curbs. Don't Autocross. Sorry, other than maintaining it like a picky girlfriend, there isn't much more to say  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000007||artherd||11-22-2001||11:17 PM||||Mobil 1 oil and filter every 3,000miles, new brake fluid every 10,000.  Like your life depends on it.<P>Grease the ball joints (Red Line makes a synthetic grease-gun grease that I swear by.)<P>All bearings on the 88 are *sealed* into complete HUB assemblies, and must be totally replaced when needed.    The rear parts are plentiful, but the fronts are out of production and HARD to find. <P>Some people re-manufacture them (you need a machine shop...)<P>Alternatively, Held Motorsports drop spindles for the 1988 cars *USE THE READIALLY AVAILABLE REAR 88 BEARINGS!!!*<P><BR>If your fronts are shot anyway, it might be a REALLY GOOD TIME to lower your car the really good way  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>If I'm not mistaken, the 88 bearings are a sealed cartridge, correct? I wasn't aware of ANY way to repack them   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>As for BJs, avoid rough roads, and hitting curbs. Don't Autocross. Sorry, other than maintaining it like a picky girlfriend, there isn't much more to say   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000008||Will||11-23-2001||12:29 AM||||A bit on '84-'87 front wheel bearings:<BR>If you take the outer bearing out and take the hub off the spindle, you can reach through the hub with a punch and drive the inner bearing out from inside. The inner bearing then drives out the seal without damaging it. If it's in good shape, the seal can then be reused.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Pheagey||11-23-2001||12:37 AM||||My peace of advice:<BR>   It or when you ever bust the boots on the axle don't get stuck in sand/dirt/mud. How do I know this? Don't ask...<BR>   O and never leave your car on the side of the road for more than 3-4 hours. My Fiero was robbed twice in two days...<BR>   Anyways....<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed;<BR>Have you seen my website yet?<BR>Http://www.LithiumEffects/pheageyfeiro<BR>"The car formaly know as, and has now reverted back to being known as, Pheagey's Fiero."||||reg||1
Z||000010||Smoooooth GT||11-23-2001||01:25 AM||||My Advice:  Don't jump your Fiero in front of your friend's when trying to prove something...   just use common sense!!!<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Smooooth_Yellow_sig_3.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/texas_1.gif">          <BR>Shaved door handles, sidetrim, exhaust outlet's, etc.... smooooth!!!||||reg||1
